Project Journal — Aakriti Sharma
Project: ReVive — Financial Anomaly Detection (UCS503 Semester Project)
Role: System Diagrams (UML)

Week 3
Focus: Building the Class Diagram

Summary
This week focused on the Class Diagram — translating the system's architecture into a proper object-oriented structure showing the core classes and how they relate to and depend on each other, which is generally the most detail-dense of the five diagrams.

What I did
●	Identified the core system classes needed to represent the full pipeline as software components: DataIngestor, Preprocessor, RepairModel (the ReVive detection engine), ScoringEngine, ExplainabilityModule, TriageClassifier, AlertRecord, and APIController.
●	Worked out the relationships between these classes — for example, that ScoringEngine consumes RepairModel's output, that TriageClassifier consumes ScoringEngine's output to decide Normal/Review/High-Risk, and that AlertRecord represents the persisted data written whenever TriageClassifier flags something.
●	Decided on appropriate class-level attributes and methods for each class at a level of detail suitable for a course diagram (not full implementation-level detail) — e.g., RepairModel with a forward()/repair() method, ScoringEngine with a compute_score() method.
●	Built the full Class Diagram showing all eight classes with their key attributes/methods and the association/dependency arrows between them.

Technical details / observations
●	Deliberately kept AlertRecord as a simple data-holding class (attributes only, minimal methods) to reflect that it represents a stored database record rather than active logic — this distinction matters for keeping the diagram an accurate reflection of the system's actual design, not just a list of every class name.
●	Named the detection engine class RepairModel in the diagram to keep it descriptive of its function, independent of whatever the underlying model implementation is called in code at any given time.

Issues / blockers
●	None blocking — the class list was stable enough by this point in the project (given the model architecture from the technical track had already been implemented the previous week) that no significant rework was needed.

Learnings
●	A Class Diagram is most useful when each class's role is kept single-purpose and clearly named (e.g., separating ScoringEngine from TriageClassifier rather than merging them) — this makes the diagram easier to explain in a viva and mirrors good software design practice.

Time spent (approx.): ~5 hours (identifying classes, working out relationships, drawing the diagram).
Plan for next week: Build the Data Flow Diagram and Sequence Diagram to complete the full required diagram set.
